#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216940 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5efaf8e-59a8-5820-88aa-22407a4ba9f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Clairaut Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "c32b13cb-016f-5d9a-a7a3-380a1363ab44" :question/math-academy-id "e-21082" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n1. $y -xy' -\\ln(y')=0$\n2. $y^4\\cos(x) +xyy' = y^2$\n3. $y= xy' + 2y'$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny = xy' + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of $y'$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y$, we get\n$y = xy' + \\ln(y')$,\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y = xy' + g(y')$ with\n$g(y') = \\ln(y')$.\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y$, we get\n$y^3\\cos(x) +xy' = y$.\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y = xy' + g(y')$.\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y = xy' + g(y')$ with\n$g(y') = 2y'$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216941 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ddcff62-d052-51fd-9e2b-50358fa02de5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "20c69b24-dd3a-5da9-a537-d296430fd050" :question/math-academy-id "e-21083" :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n$y = xy' + (y')^2+1$." :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny = xy' + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of $y'$.\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + (y')^2 +1\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p = y'$.\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = xp + p^2+1\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= (xp + p^{2} + 1)^{′} \\\\\np &= p + xp^{′} + 2pp^{′} \\\\\n0 &= p^{′}(x + 2p)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if\n\n$$\nx + 2p = 0\n$$\n\nThen,\n$x = -2p$.\n\nNow, let's substitute this into\n\n$$\ny = xp + p^2+1{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= xp + p^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= (-2p)p + p^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&=-2p^{2} + p^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&=-p^{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{x =-2p; y =-p^{2} + 1\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p$, we first solve the first equation for $p{:}$\n\n$$\np = -\\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -p^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= - (-\\frac{x}{2})^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{x^{2}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny = 1- \\dfrac{x^2}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aac5cb30-5659-547e-bedf-921f6c99067a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "c85481b8-a35a-58ff-a820-97d1988b604b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21413" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n$y = xy' + e^{-y'}$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + e^{-y'}\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p = y'$.\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = x p + e^{-p}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= (xp + e^{-p})^{′} \\\\\np &= p + xp^{′} - e^{-p}p^{′} \\\\\n0 &= p^{′}(x - e^{-p})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $p' = 0$. Then, $p$ is constant, say\n$p = c$.\n\nSubstituting $p = c$ into\n\n$$\ny = x p + e^{-p}\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution\n\n$$\ny = cx + e^{-c}\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217724 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5efaf8e-59a8-5820-88aa-22407a4ba9f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Clairaut Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2b6e78e-c21c-538f-af2b-256c666f3756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216442} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb1b211-d39f-5593-aadb-18c79b53d3d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216443} {:answer/id #uuid "fbaef773-3fe4-5750-b33b-3c84c6e86737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216444} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb917bf-0cbc-52f6-a2ca-d6b2811a7472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216445} {:answer/id #uuid "79c9d834-8596-5593-a7e4-43e0aae5f01d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbaef773-3fe4-5750-b33b-3c84c6e86737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216444} :answer-field/id #uuid "f25d499a-4b46-5410-807f-c624550d9826" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216441}] :question/id #uuid "ecc8e10a-18c5-5bba-b6c4-e2566c05f80d" :question/math-academy-id "q-321228" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n1. $y = xy^{′} + (y^{′})^{3}$\n2. $yy^{′} - xy^{3} + 2xy^{2} = \\sin (x)y$\n3. $xy^{′} + e^{y^{′}} - y = 0$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ddcff62-d052-51fd-9e2b-50358fa02de5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edd2154f-9f8e-57db-931e-edd4f6b3a09e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\ln (-x) - 1, x < 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216448} {:answer/id #uuid "15304641-9a3a-5929-841e-82af3e8567e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x(\\ln (x) + 1), x > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216449} {:answer/id #uuid "2f28213f-c86c-5448-9006-c4815c68e91c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x(\\ln (-x) - 1), x < 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216450} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc4de28-729f-55b3-a418-40170dddb03f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{2}(\\ln (-x) - 1), x < 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216451} {:answer/id #uuid "74cdac0e-87f1-5453-8c2a-718b4badbebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x(\\ln (x) - 1), x > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f28213f-c86c-5448-9006-c4815c68e91c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x(\\ln (-x) - 1), x < 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216450} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e02ee69-562b-5751-b845-7ddb6ef9fc32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216447}] :question/id #uuid "3ecd5c87-7f22-51ab-9317-a294ec7da02c" :question/math-academy-id "q-321239" :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$y = xy^{′} + e^{y^{′}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aac5cb30-5659-547e-bedf-921f6c99067a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e8d0f94-09f7-58f3-bf0c-0